<!--
  Delta-38 pusher propeller  [Q 4.8 name = "uav_prop"]
  20 in = 0.508 m, 2 blades, fixed pitch, direct drive.

    diameter   0.508 m  [Q 6.1]
    fixed      yes      [Q 6.2]  => minpitch = maxpitch, constspeed = 0
    numblades  2        [Q 6.3]
    gearratio  1.0      [Q 6.4]  no reduction: prop rpm = engine rpm
    minrpm/maxrpm       [Q 6.5] "same as the engine" => 3000 / 6300
    ixx        [Q 6.6] left to the engineer - see below
    Ct/Cp      [Q 6.7] left to the engineer - see below

  SIZING THE Ct/Cp TABLES
  =======================
  The tables have to satisfy two hard constraints at once:

  (1) POWER ABSORPTION. To absorb the engine's rated 5.22 kW at its 6300 rpm
      redline [Q 5.1/5.2] on a 0.508 m disc:
        Cp = P / (rho n^3 D^5) = 5220 / (1.225 * 105^3 * 0.508^5) = 0.109
      That is a high Cp for a 2-blade prop, and it is the real message of the
      questionnaire's propulsion numbers: 5.2 kW through a 20 in direct-drive
      2-blade disc needs a WIDE-CHORD blade (a Mejzlik/Biela-class UAV prop),
      not a thin hobby prop. A standard 20x10 sport prop would absorb barely
      1.5 kW at these revs and the engine would simply overspeed.
      See DATA-GAPS.md item 3.

  (2) STATIC THRUST. Momentum theory with a figure of merit of 0.55 on the
      same disc and power:
        T0 = (2 rho A)^(1/3) * (0.55 P)^(2/3) = 160 N
      => Ct0 = T0/(rho n^2 D^4) = 0.178.

  Both constraints are met at J=0: Cp=0.109, Ct=0.178. The curves then use
  standard 2-blade fixed-pitch shapes (McCormick sec.9.4; Hartman and Biermann
  NACA TR-640) with an effective pitch/D ~ 0.7 (14 in), giving:
      peak efficiency  eta = J*Ct/Cp = 0.71 at J = 0.70
      zero thrust      J = 1.02        (windmill onset)
      negative Ct AND negative Cp beyond that, so the prop brakes and drives
      the engine in a dive instead of endlessly loading it. (The Geran-2 file
      documents why a monotonically increasing Cp, which is what aeromatic
      emits, prevents the engine reaching its design rpm at all.)

  OPERATING POINTS - MEASURED in standalone JSBSim with the real engine file
  (scripts/d38_cruise.xml; rho n^2 D^4 = 900 N at 6300 rpm):
      static, full throttle   6353 rpm  6.95 hp   157 N   3.7 L/h
      34 m/s, full throttle   6350 rpm  6.94 hp   104 N   3.7 L/h
      34 m/s, 80% throttle    5388 rpm  3.78 hp    65 N   2.2 L/h  <== cruise
      max climb (31 m/s FT)   6400 rpm  6.9  hp   110 N   +4.6 m/s
      level flight tops out at ~40 m/s / 145 km/h at full throttle.
  The engine therefore sits AT its 6300 rpm redline at full throttle across the
  whole speed range, which is what a correctly matched fixed-pitch prop does.
  Tip Mach at redline = pi*D*n/a = 0.49, so the Mach corrections never bite.

  BLADE LOADING - WHAT Cp = 0.109 ACTUALLY IMPLIES
    A thin sport propeller of this diameter absorbs roughly
        P[W] = 5.3e-15 * D^4 * pitch * rpm^3   (D, pitch in inches)
    which for a 20x10 at 6300 rpm is only ~2.1 kW, i.e. Cp = 0.044. Reaching
    Cp = 0.109 at the same diameter and rpm needs about 2.5x that blade
    loading. A wide-chord UAV propeller (Mejzlik / Biela / Xoar PJN class,
    roughly twice the blade chord of an APC "E") at 13-15 in of pitch gets
    there; a thin hobby 20x10 would not, and the engine would simply overspeed
    past its redline instead of making rated power. minpitch/maxpitch below is
    set to the equivalent 24 deg at 0.75R and is cosmetic - with `internal`
    Ct/Cp tables indexed on J alone, FGPropeller does not use it for a
    fixed-pitch prop. See DATA-GAPS.md item 3: the real propeller's make and
    model would replace these curves with measured data.

  NEGATIVE BRANCH (J beyond zero-thrust)
    Both curves cross zero near J = 1.2-1.45 and go negative, so in a dive the
    prop brakes and drives the engine rather than endlessly loading it. (The
    Geran-2 file documents why a monotonically increasing Cp - which is what
    aeromatic emits - stops the engine reaching its design rpm at all.) The
    negative tail was halved from the first draft after a measured closed-
    throttle descent: Ct = -0.235 at J = 2.0 added ~60 N of prop drag, roughly
    doubling total airframe drag, which is more braking than a two-blade
    fixed-pitch prop of this solidity actually produces.

  ROTATIONAL INERTIA [Q 6.6, "engineer estimates it"]
    20 in carbon 2-blade, ~0.35 kg/blade, treated as a slender rod about the
    hub: Ixx = 2 * m r^2/3 = 2*0.35*0.254^2/3 = 0.0151 kg m2; +30% for the hub
    and spinner = 0.0196 kg m2 = 0.0145 slug ft2.
-->
<propeller version="1.01" name="uav_prop">

